sample_texts = [
    "Typing is an essential skill in today’s digital world. Whether you're writing emails, coding, or messaging friends, fast and accurate typing saves time and enhances communication. Many people start typing with two fingers, often referred to as 'hunt and peck'. However, with a little bit of daily practice, anyone can improve. The key to typing faster is muscle memory, which develops over time. You must position your fingers properly and resist the urge to look at the keyboard. Practicing with real-world texts or using typing games can make learning more enjoyable. Just fifteen minutes per day can lead to noticeable improvement.",
    "Learning to type properly is one of the best investments you can make in your productivity. Professional typists can exceed 100 words per minute, allowing them to complete writing tasks much faster than average users. If you’re working in programming, journalism, or any role that involves constant communication, typing proficiency becomes even more important. It’s not just about speed—it’s also about accuracy. Every typo introduces friction into your workflow. When you minimize errors, you save time otherwise spent editing. So, stay consistent with your practice. Track your progress and celebrate small gains to stay motivated. Remember, slow is smooth, smooth is fast.",
    "In the early days of computing, typing was primarily a skill for secretaries and writers. But today, nearly every profession requires some form of digital communication. That’s why typing should be taught in schools alongside reading and writing. A fast and accurate typist can perform better academically and professionally. When typing becomes second nature, you’re free to focus on ideas instead of mechanics. Whether writing an essay, drafting a proposal, or coding a website, proficient typing boosts your efficiency. Fortunately, tools like online typing tutors and desktop applications make it easier than ever to practice. Start slow, stay steady, and success will follow.",
    "Typing speed isn’t just about hammering the keyboard quickly. It’s a delicate balance between speed and precision. If you type too fast and make many errors, you’ll waste time correcting your mistakes. Conversely, typing slowly but accurately can lead to consistent improvement. Set a daily goal and challenge yourself to improve your average words per minute over time. When typing, use all ten fingers, keep your wrists elevated, and relax your shoulders. Avoid slouching or craning your neck. Physical comfort is essential for long typing sessions. Like playing the piano, touch typing is an art that rewards discipline and patience.",
    "One of the best ways to improve typing skills is to copy long passages of text. Choose texts that interest you—stories, news articles, or even coding snippets. This practice helps with rhythm, spelling, and mental endurance. If you’re using a typing app, try increasing the difficulty level gradually. Begin with simple sentences and move to complex paragraphs. As you build confidence, avoid looking at the keyboard. Your brain will start recognizing patterns, and your hands will learn to find keys automatically. Review your stats after each session and identify common errors. This targeted practice leads to more meaningful improvement.",
    "In today’s fast-paced world, communication happens at the speed of thought. If your typing can’t keep up, you may find yourself falling behind. Whether you’re a student taking notes in class, a developer writing code, or an employee composing emails, fast and accurate typing enhances your productivity. It reduces the mental strain of switching between thought and expression. Practice daily using lessons that focus on individual keys, then words, and finally full sentences. Test yourself under timed conditions. Repetition is your best friend. With dedication, you’ll build muscle memory and confidence, making typing a seamless part of your digital life.",
    "The journey to becoming a fast typist can feel long, but every minute you practice brings you closer to mastery. Begin with finger placement—each finger has a home row and specific responsibilities. Train yourself not to look at the keys, even if it slows you down at first. Over time, your speed will increase naturally. Focus on posture: sit upright, with elbows at 90 degrees, and wrists floating slightly above the keyboard. Avoid resting your palms. Practice with a metronome to develop rhythm and consistency. Don’t just type random words—use real text to build context and comprehension as well.",
    "Many people underestimate the power of good typing habits. When you type efficiently, your mind is free to think creatively and critically. This is especially true for writers and programmers who need to translate complex thoughts into words or code. The less you think about the act of typing, the more you can focus on problem-solving and expression. Try setting daily goals like 'type 500 words without error' or 'maintain 95% accuracy for 5 minutes'. Use these goals to build consistency. And don’t get discouraged by plateaus. Improvement often comes in waves, and every session you complete lays the foundation for the next.",
    "Typing is more than a mechanical skill—it’s a mental exercise. As you learn to type faster, you also train your brain to process information more quickly. Think of your keyboard as an extension of your mind. Just like musicians learn scales and chords, typists develop muscle memory through structured drills. Speed comes with time, but accuracy must be prioritized from the beginning. Pay attention to punctuation and spelling, as these affect the clarity of your writing. Use error analysis to spot recurring mistakes and focus on fixing them. Remember, fast typing with frequent errors is like running with untied shoes.",
    "Whether you're typing an essay, writing code, or chatting with a friend, the ability to do it quickly and correctly makes a huge difference. The average person types around 40 words per minute, but with practice, reaching 70 or even 100 WPM is achievable. Practice is key. Try daily timed tests, typing books, or copying your favorite articles. Keep a log of your stats to see improvement over time. If possible, use a mechanical keyboard to enhance feedback and precision. The goal isn't just speed—it's fluency. Fluency means you don’t think about typing at all; it just flows, naturally and effortlessly.",
]
